The response of the detector to incident charged pions was studied using a 5 GeV/c pion beam. Since we are using pion beams in the relativistic range, they ionize the gas depending upon the thickness of the gaseous medium while traversing through the detector and they can be considered as, Minimum Ionizing Particles (MIPs). Typical pulse height spectra for a 5 GeV/c pion beam are shown in fig 3.13 along with fits to Landau distribution for a wide range of operating voltage. It is seen that the value of mean, most probable value (MPV) and RMS of the distributions increase with the high voltage. The distributions are obtained by making clusters by adding central cell to the nearest neighbouring cells. In Fig. 3.13 clear Landau type distributions are visible starting from 1200 to 1400 Volt. Fig 3.14 gives the average cluster size due to pions. The average cell multiplicity for pions being close to unity

